 \section{Research design and research questions}
 \label{sec:ch4-design}
-
-REASSEMBLE was the main study of multimodal fusion. Its hand camera and machine sensors
-recorded the same robotic actions, and each completed action had a recorded outcome.
-This made it possible to ask three questions on the same cases. How well could the
-camera predict failure on its own? How well could the sensors predict it? Did combining
-the two improve on the stronger individual predictor?
-
-The other studies tested related questions under different conditions. PHM established
-target-validity and modality-correspondence limits while combining sensor groups to
-predict a photographic proxy. MSM supplied the primary completed external manufacturing
-comparison: locally fitted visual, sensor and fusion models, six target/grouping
-sensitivities, and controlled corruption tests. ViFailback added task-disjoint robotic
-assessment and transfer of a specified REASSEMBLE-learned visual representation; target-CNN
-adaptation remained partial and its corruption study was unexecuted. HANDOVER's official
-test split supported unimodal models and fixed averaging, while missing participant
-identities prevented the required participant-safe OOF fits for learned fusion. That
-supporting study is retained in Appendix~\ref{app:handover}.
-
-The five research requirements guided how these tests were read. \textbf{R1} concerned
-the target and the pairing of observations. \textbf{R2} concerned how each input was
-represented. \textbf{R3} asked what fusion added over a strong single input. \textbf{R4}
-concerned the response to missing or damaged inputs. \textbf{R5} concerned the strength
-of the evidence, including the choice of test groups, the uncertainty of the results,
-and the cost of the method.
+\label{sec:literature-study-gaps}
+
+The welding studies in Chapter~\ref{ch:literature} give different answers about the
+value of a second input. Figure~\ref{fig:fusion-research-gap} shows how those
+comparisons led to five questions for reading the empirical evidence. The
+requirements organize the historical evidence; they do not imply that every
+experiment was prospectively designed around this framework.
+
+\begin{figure}[htbp]
+  \centering
+  \caption{From welding evidence to requirements for testing fusion}
+  \label{fig:ch02-synthesis-requirements}
+  \label{fig:fusion-research-gap}
+  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fusion_research_gap.pdf}
+  \displaynote{DMAW reported 96.1\% accuracy for fusion and 90.2\% for
+    its strongest single input \parencite{P003}. In weld penetration, both
+    single-input models and fusion reached 100\% on the clean test set
+    \parencite{Hua2026Weld}. The reported contrasts are within each study.}
+\end{figure}
+
+\textbf{R1. What is predicted, and when?} A camera can supply a predictor or a
+reference measured after the event. For each study, we identify the physical event,
+which observations describe that event, when each was available, and how the target
+was obtained. In PHM, post-run photographs supply a proxy for gear condition. In
+REASSEMBLE, the camera and sensors are predictors paired with a recorded action
+outcome. A readable image or aligned timestamp alone does not establish target
+validity or physical correspondence.
+
+\textbf{R2. What does each representation retain (SQ1)?} Published results for
+engineered features, pretrained visual features and temporal patches concern
+different targets and fitting conditions
+\parencite{Horwitz2023Back,Roth2022Towards,Nie2023PatchTST}. We compare individual
+branches on the same target and observation window, recording their training
+histories and fitting budgets. A branch's standalone score leaves open whether it
+contributes information to another predictor.
+
+\textbf{R3. What does fusion add (SQ2)?} The clean welding tie and the loss after
+adding gripper state in handover make the strongest single input an essential
+comparator \parencite{Hua2026Weld,Thoduka2024Handover}. On the 4,530 eligible
+REASSEMBLE actions, we compare camera and sensor predictions, fixed averaging and
+fitted combinations with the same recordings held out. The feature-level model F3
+projects a 128-dimensional visual representation and 220 sensor statistics to two
+128-dimensional vectors and concatenates them. This is distinct from combining
+two representations of the sensor stream. Matched opportunities for fitting and
+recalibration are needed to isolate the contribution of an additional input.
+Chapter~\ref{ch:reassemble} gives the fitting and calibration differences that
+limit attribution to the fusion rule alone.
+
+\textbf{R4. Which reliability condition is being tested (SQ3)?} Knowing an input is
+absent calls for a different response from receiving a degraded but apparently
+available signal \parencite{P026,Zhang2023QMF}. We identify the affected input and
+what the model knows about its condition. In the matched REASSEMBLE comparison,
+static and adaptive models receive comparable corruption exposure during fitting.
+Both are assessed on clean and corrupted cases. Timing changes and transfer to
+another domain are separate tests.
+
+\textbf{R5. How dependable is the comparison (SQ4)?} Selection on related
+observations can inflate performance estimates \parencite{P004,P017}. The
+assessment unit follows the acquisition process, such as a gear experiment,
+recording, or manipulation task. Ranking, probability calibration and uncertainty
+answer different questions about failure prediction. Fitting and inference costs
+also need a stated measurement boundary. Across datasets, we distinguish refitting
+a procedure, transferring representation weights and testing an unchanged
+predictor. Table~\ref{tab:fusion-study-scope} identifies which comparisons the
+available data support; investigating a requirement does not mean that every
+condition needed for a strong claim was satisfied.
+
+The published tasks help define the boundaries of the five investigations.
+Post-build CT can supply an independent physical reference in manufacturing
+\parencite{Petrich2021Multimodal}; PHM instead uses periodic photographs and a
+sensor-only deployment specification \parencite{PHM2026Challenge}. Milling studies
+of wear and cutting force \parencites[Tables~IV--V]{P021}[Table~1]{P045}
+motivate the value of additional
+observations, while MSM asks whether video adds information about abnormal cutting
+beyond machine telemetry \parencite{Kim2026MSM}. REASSEMBLE's published segmentation
+and visual condition-check benchmarks \parencite{Sliwowski2025REASSEMBLE} differ
+from the completed-action failure target here. Published handover trials establish
+a five-class outcome benchmark \parencite{Thoduka2024Handover}; the supplementary
+thesis study instead compares binary failure predictions and their fixed average.
+ViFailback's published visual-symbol diagnosis and corrective-action task
+\parencite[Tables~2--4]{Zeng2026ViFailback} differs from the binary episode-outcome comparison
+here: the latter tests generic and transferred visual features against robot state.
+These differences determine the targets and controls; they do not establish a
+common cross-dataset leaderboard.
+
